# --- patterns as learnable content (GDD economy: harder base pays more, so one
# tamed routine is never optimal). P cycles the unlocked patterns in training.
# "mult" is `s` in the segment formula: it multiplies a WHOLE clean stretch, not a
# catch. Louis's values, 2026-07-29: Cascade 1, Columns 1.2, Half Shower 2, Reverse
# Cascade 1.5, Tennis 1.5, and the Shower is 3 (he named it on 2026-07-29 evening - it is
# the biggest coefficient in the table, above the Half Shower's 2).
# EXACTLY ONE pattern is active at a time (Louis): Reverse Cascade and Tennis live
# HERE now, not as Space toggles, so switching patterns always switches the whole
# routine and Columns can never run "on top of" anything.
# Unlocks are spread across the CAREER, not the first evenings (Louis): each entry
# may ask for a level, a stat, and a stage. All present keys must be met -
# see skill_req_met / skill_req_text.
# Waterfall (2026-07-19, was index 6) was REMOVED (Louis: "identical to half
# shower"), and Fountain (2026-07-19 later, was index 1) was REMOVED too (Louis:
# "fountain isn't a pattern, that's just cascade for 4 balls") - CASCADE alone now
# covers both roles, crossing at an odd count and keeping each hand's own circle
# at an even one (do_throw's `cross`), with one shared mult, no separate entry.
# Indices are NOT frozen any more (both removals needed real migration) - see
# load_progress's versioned pattern_sel migration for the full history.
# "room" is how much DAYLIGHT the pattern's own roads leave between props, 1.0 being a
# Cascade's, and it only ever scales the HITBOX (never a drawn size, never the score).
# Measured, not guessed: `--collcheck --latecatch 4 --maxstats` at 4/5/6 objects reports
# how close the ideal pattern actually brings two props, and these are those numbers.
# The spread is enormous - Columns leaves ~40px, Tennis and Half Shower leave ~2px,
# because both fly an over-the-top road straight through the weave they cross - so one
# count-only hitbox cannot be fair to all six: sized for Columns it would clack every
# clean Tennis, and sized for Tennis it forgives everything. Hence: forgiveness follows
# the room the pattern leaves. Half Shower's and Tennis's ~0.05 is the honest reading of
# a pattern whose roads genuinely intersect under real (not frame-perfect) timing - a
# long-standing residual, documented under the arms rule in CLAUDE.md, that the game
# must not punish the player for.
const PATTERNS := TOSSA_CATALOG.PATTERNS
# UNLOCK DESIGN (Louis): every skill sits behind a wall of difficulty, hard work or
# talent - never behind raw XP alone. Each NEW STAGE guarantees exactly one arrival
# reward, gated on the venue and nothing else (Street: Behind the Back. Theatre: Spin.
# Circus: Shower. Championship: Reverse Cascade. World Arena: Tennis), so moving up
# always pays. The rest carry composite stat/level walls and fill the stretches
# BETWEEN stages; when even those run dry for too long, the pity director in
# enter_day gifts the nearest one (see pity_grant).
# THE GAME'S NAME, IN ONE PLACE (2026-07-28, Louis: "replace IACTO with _TITLE_ (or
# something) so that i can easily change it later"). Change these two and every readout,
# the wordmark, the daily share line, the credits and the demo wall's call to action all
# follow. They are two constants rather than one because the WORDMARK letters the name
# differently from the way it is written: a real juggling ball stands in for the name's
# LAST letter, so the mark needs the name without it. TOSSA is drawn "TOSS" plus a ball
# for the a; under the old name IACTO it was "I ACT" plus a ball for the o, which is
# where the two-constant shape came from.
# MOVING THE CAPSULE MARK AROUND (2026-07-28, Louis: "is it possible for me to move
# around the objects? and how"). Yes: everything the picture places is one of the numbers
# below, and nothing else needs touching. Re-render with
#     godot --path . --resolution 1232x706 -- --capsule 4 --shot out.png --shotat 40
#
# CAP_NUDGE has one row per LETTER OF THE NAME, in order, so the two S's move
# independently: x, y, scale. x and y are in units of the mark's own height (0.1 is a
# tenth of the letter height, positive y is DOWN), and scale multiplies that one letter.
# A letter's slot widens with its scale, so making one bigger pushes the others aside
# instead of overlapping them.
