#
# THE RENAME COST ALMOST NOTHING BECAUSE THE LOGO NEVER SAID PIXELTOSSA. MARK_WORD has
# spelled TOSSA in props since the fork began (see below: P, I, X, E and L have no honest
# prop shape, so the mark could never have carried them), so the three baked wordmark
# sprites are correct as they stand and no re-bake was needed. Only the TEXT name moved.
#
# GAME_TITLE is the game's name as TEXT: the window title, the credits, the daily share
# string, "WISHLIST %s ON STEAM".
#
# MARK_WORD is what the PROP WORDMARK SPELLS, and it is deliberately NOT the name. The
# mark has no lettering in it at all - capsule_mark builds each glyph out of juggling
# props (T = two knives, O = a ring, S = a line of balls, A = two leaning clubs), so its
# `match ch` knows exactly four letters and every other character falls to `_: pass`,
# drawing nothing while still advancing the cursor by cap_letter_width's 0.60 default.
# CAP_NUDGE compounds it: five hand-tuned entries indexed BY POSITION, so a longer word
# slides the T's 1.10 scale onto some other letter and leaves the real letters untuned.
# Spelling PIXELTOSSA in props would therefore have drawn five blank gaps followed by a
# mis-scaled TOSSA - not a layout problem, a missing-glyph one. P, I, X, E and L would
# each need designing out of props first, and P and E have no honest prop shape.
#
# The lockup WAS "PIXEL" set in the pixel font above the five-prop TOSSA mark. It is not
# any more (2026-08-08, Louis: "the titlescreen should not have PIXEL above tossa") - the
# logo is the props alone, everywhere it appears: title screen, key art, credits and all
# five store capsules. The mark keeps its tuning table, its measured widths and its
# MARK_RISE/MARK_DROP arithmetic exactly as they always were.
#
# TITLE_MARK keeps its documented meaning (the name without its final round letter, the
# ball standing in for that letter). It has no call sites today; it is kept in step with
# GAME_TITLE so it is correct if one appears.
# NAMES STILL ON THE TABLE (Louis, 2026-07-28) - kept here beside the switch so choosing
# one is a two-line edit and never a hunt. The second column is what TITLE_MARK becomes:
# the ball prop stands in for the name's LAST letter, so the mark is the name without it,
# spaced however the joke wants to read.
#     TOSSA      "TOSS"        the ball is the final a
#     VOLO       "VOL"         (Latin, "I fly" - the same first-person as I ACT)
#     CASCADA    "CASCAD"
#     ORBITA     "ORBIT"       the ball as the thing in orbit is almost too neat
#     CATCHUP    "CATCH U"     awkward: the ball would stand for a p, not a round letter
#     AIRTIME    "AIRTIM"      same problem, ends on e
#     LAST CATCH "LAST CATC"   same problem, ends on h
#     JUGGLE RUN "JUGGLE RU"   same problem, ends on n
#     CASCADE    "CASCAD"      ends on e, but "CASCAD" + a ball reads as CASCADA
# The wordmark only really sings when the name ENDS IN A ROUND LETTER (o or a), which is
# what IACTO, TOSSA, VOLO, CASCADA and ORBITA all do. For any of the others the ball
# would have to sit beside the word rather than inside it, which is a different mark.
# THE PASS IS A SNAP, NOT A LOB (2026-07-28 evening, Louis: "the bottom arc of the shower
# pattern goes way too slow, the time in the air is more than triple what it should be").
# It was 0.5 of a tap - measured, 55 ticks at five objects and 67 at seven, i.e. almost half
# a second for a prop to travel sideways between two hands that are 154px apart. That is not
# a pass, it is a second throw, and it is why the low road looked like it was crawling while
# the high road ran at speed.
#
# 0.16 is his "more than triple" taken at its word: 17 ticks at five objects, 21 at seven.
# Fast, and correct - the return in a real shower is the quick half of the cycle, which is
# what makes the high hand the busy one.
#
# **THE HEIGHT IS HELD WHERE HE ALREADY APPROVED IT.** arc = ARC_PER_AIR x air, so cutting
# the airtime to a third would have taken the arc down with it, from 23px to 7 - a pass so
# flat it reads as the prop sliding sideways rather than being thrown. He asked about TIME;
# the look of the pass was settled earlier the same day and must not move as a side effect.
# SHOWER_FEED_ARC is raised to compensate exactly, so the measured peak stays ~23px.
const SHOWER_FEED := 0.16     # the feed pass's airtime as a fraction of a tap
const SHOWER_FEED_ARC := 0.63 # ...and how much of its arc it actually rises. Height and
							   # airtime are otherwise ONE number here (arc = ARC_PER_AIR x
							   # air), so the arc is the only half that can move alone -
							   # which is exactly what a horizontal pass is: quick across,
							   # almost no rise.
const SHOWER_MIN := 0.55      # the high hand's minimum height fraction in a shower
const HALFSHOWER_MIN := 0.5   # the over-the-top side's minimum height fraction (0.35
							   # left its arc only ~34px above the weave where they
							   # cross; 0.5 keeps clear air between the two roads)
# THE LOW ROAD IS A MULTIPLE OF THE TAP, NEVER THE TAP PLUS SOMETHING (2026-07-28,
# Louis: "for many objects, you throw the lower throw so high, that it collides with the
# upper throw, although the fysics don't measure the collision"). He is right, and the
# reason is arithmetic. The cap used to be `base_air + 0.2 * a full hold`, and base_air
# scales with the count (count_air_scale, 1.0 at four objects up to 2.8 at fifteen), so
# the constant part stopped mattering: measured at nine objects the "low" road peaked
# 379px against the high road's 433px, i.e. 88% of the height of the throw it exists to
# duck under. The hitbox audit never saw it because hitboxes shrink with the count while
# the DRAWN props do not, which is exactly the collision Louis can see and --collcheck
# cannot. A MULTIPLE of base_air holds its proportion at every count, which is what the
# Shower's feed always did - hence his "you have made shower do a perfect half shower".
# So this is the Shower's feed value plus the 30% he asked for on top.
# **AND IT IS ITS OWN NUMBER NOW, not a multiple of the Shower's.** It was
# `SHOWER_FEED * 1.3`, which was true and tidy right up until the Shower's feed was cut to a
# third: that one edit would have silently dragged the Half Shower's low road down with it,
# from 0.65 to 0.21, wrecking a pattern Louis had measured and approved and had said nothing
# about. **A constant derived from another constant is a coupling, and a coupling between two
# patterns that are tuned separately is a bug waiting for one of them to be retuned.** The
# value is unchanged; only its independence is new.
const HALFSHOWER_LOW := 0.65   # the weave side's airtime as a fraction of a tap, which keeps
							   # its arc near half the high road's from three objects to
							   # fifteen (measured 0.43 to 0.55)
const OVER_TOP_MIN := 0.5     # generic over-the-top minimum height fraction (Overthrow,
							   # Reverse Cascade, Tennis's marked ball)
const TENNIS_MIN := OVER_TOP_MIN * 1.2   # Tennis's marked prop goes 20% higher than a
							   # plain over-the-top lob (2026-07-28, Louis) - it is the one
							   # prop crossing over a full weave rather than one road
# WHERE A COLUMN MAY STAND (2026-07-28, Louis: every object goes straight up and comes
# down exactly where it was thrown, and "for many objects ... nor does it widen the arms
# positioning (wider looks bad)"). Both rules are geometry, so the lane band is defined
# by what the STANCE can already reach and never by the count:
#   - the innermost lane sits outside the torso (~25px of half-width), so the hand that
#     throws it is never drawn inside the chest and never has to be clamped away from
#     its own lane - the clamp is what used to make a ball leap sideways out of the hand
#   - the outermost lane sits exactly on the outer catch x (HAND_OUT_DX), the widest the
#     arms EVER reach in any pattern, so Columns can never be the thing that splays them
# The lanes on one side then spread evenly inside that band, so the gap between two
# lanes falls like 1/count - which is the same curve prop_hit_scale's hitbox already
# falls on, and why the clearance stays roughly constant from 3 objects to 15.
# THE STANCE, as two numbers instead of two loose literals inside inner_pos/outer_pos:
# everything that has to stay INSIDE the arms' reach (the Columns lane band below) reads
# them, so the stance and the things measured against it can never drift apart.
# HOW MUCH HIGHER THE INWARD HAND SITS (2026-08-07, Louis: "the inwards hand position should
# have a slight inward lower arm bend in the elbow with the hand and its palm coming higher").
#
# THIS IS WHAT THE ART ALREADY WANTED. Every generated animation raises the palm as the arm
# tucks - measured across all nineteen of them, the palm sits at 58-65% of the figure's height
# at the inner reach against 49-51% at the outer, because that is what a person's forearm does
# when it folds in. The bank used to fight it: the fitter demanded one flat hand line and the
# inner entries were accepted only on a loosened tolerance, i.e. the pose was tolerated rather
# than wanted. Lifting the slot to meet the drawing makes the mechanic and the picture agree,
# and the elbow bend comes free because it is painted into the frames that now qualify.
#
# ...AND IT IS ZERO AGAIN, BECAUSE THE MEASUREMENT IT WAS FITTED TO WAS MEASURING THE ELBOW
# (2026-08-08). The 42px above was chosen to lift the game's inner slot up to where the
# painting was believed to put its palm at close reach - 63.5% of the way up the figure. That
# belief came from armlib.palms, which called a palm "the moving pixel furthest from the
# centreline", and when a forearm folds the furthest moving pixel is the ELBOW. Measured
# properly - by finding the blob of his own skin, which is a hand and cannot be an elbow - the
# palm at inner reach sits at 47.8% and the whole 0..44 range sits between 48% and 53%. There
# is no lift in the painting to meet.
#
# WHAT IT COST WHILE IT WAS WRONG is worth naming, because it did not look like a geometry
# bug: inner_pos is where a prop is HELD and thrown from, so every held object was drawn 42
# screen px above the hand holding it - up at the waistcoat, which is exactly Louis's "the
# objects don't match the hand placement". Keep it in step with LIFT_FLAT in
# build_arm_bank.py: the two describe the same hand line from the two ends of the seam.
# ...AND IT IS A DIP NOW, NOT A LIFT (2026-08-12, Louis's call). Both slots sat on one dead-level
# line, so a hand slid in and out horizontally like a telescope and the painted arm - which has no
# choice but to follow it - had to swing its shoulder 69 degrees to cover the travel, because the
# joint is only 18 art px above that line and reaching inward along it folds the chain almost in
# half. A real cascade does not do that: the hand carries DOWN and inward from the catch and comes
# back up to throw. Dipping the inner slot matches the picture to the pattern and more than halves
# the shoulder swing (69 -> 32 deg, elbow travel 22 -> 11 art px, both measured).
#
# Keep it in step with HAND_CARRY_DIP in build_arm_rig.py - the two describe the same hand line
# from the two ends of the seam, in screen px here and art px there, so this is exactly twice
# that number. Out of step, `fig_hand_pos`'s FIG_PALM_MAX guard starts refusing painted palms and
# props snap back to the simulated hand mid-run.
const HAND_IN_DIP := 26.0
const HAND_IN_DX := 54.0      # inner_pos: just outside the torso, where a prop is held
const HAND_OUT_DX := 100.0    # outer_pos: the catch point, and the widest the arms go
const COL_LANE_IN := 44.0     # innermost lane, measured from the centre line
