# you switch on, throw, and switch off; "arm" is the one-shot powerup (Overthrow
# only), one press empowers exactly the next throw; "live" acts on the balls
# already airborne (Spin). Each landed shaped catch pays its bonus, so a trick
# is a skill choice, never a free bonus. A running toggle SURVIVES an R restart
# in training, so a trick can be drilled run after run. Unlocks are spread over
# the career like the patterns' (lvl / str / acc / ven keys, all must be met).
# THESE MUST MATCH THE TABLE BELOW, INDEX FOR INDEX (fixed 2026-07-27, Louis: "in the
# trick menu; behind the back and high toss are swapped"). They did not: TR_HIGH was 0
# and TR_BTB 1, while TRICKS[0] is "btb" and TRICKS[1] is "high". Because these are used
# as TRICKS indices AND as the tags on a throw, every rule keyed to one was landing on
# the other - `_btb_behind` sent the HIGH TOSS behind the body, and the 1.8 held-height
# ceiling was applied to BEHIND THE BACK. Nothing was mislabelled; the two tricks had
# genuinely swapped mechanics. Add a trick and this pairing is the first thing to check.
const TR_BTB := 0
const TR_HIGH := 1
const TR_SPIN := 2
# OVERTHROW IS GONE (2026-07-28, Louis: "remove overthrow as trick"). It was the last
# "arm" trick - the kind that primes one throw and pays off when that throw is caught -
# and it was the odd one out for a reason worth writing down: what it DID (send one prop
# over the top of the pattern) is not a trick, it is what Reverse Cascade, Tennis, the
# Shower and the Half Shower already are. Selling the same road twice, once as a pattern
# with a score multiplier and once as a trick with a flat bonus, is the "heavier club"
# mistake this project already rejected for props.
# The over-the-top MACHINERY stays exactly where it is: the patterns above are built on
# it. Only the trick that duplicated them is removed.
# `diff` IS THE EXPONENT'S BASE - the p in Louis's own p^x. It is what one landing of this
# trick multiplies the whole current run by, before the context scaling in trick_difficulty()
# lifts it for a hard prop, a big count and a demanding pattern.
#
# **EVERY TRICK PAYS PER ACTIVATION - ONE PRESS, ONE MULTIPLICATION** (2026-07-29, Louis:
# "different stages scale exponential, but each stage has linear scoring"). This is the change
# that makes that sentence true, and it took two goes to see. A toggle shapes every throw while
# it runs, so paying it per CATCH made x the length of the act - an exponential in TIME, and
# therefore exponential scoring *within* a stage, which is exactly what he does not want. Paid
# per activation, x is the number of times the player pressed the key: something they choose,
# bounded by attention and risk rather than by the clock. The score inside a run is then
# (linear in time) x (a multiplier you built), which is Balatro's shape - the build sets the
# scale, the accumulation is flat, and the exponential lives across antes.
#
# Because x is small again, p is large again: these are what a trick is worth ONCE.
#
# `cascade_only` is Louis's gating: "make tricks only work on the cascade pattern, except high
# toss and spin which work everywhere". It reads well because Cascade is the one pattern with
# no shape of its own to protect - every other pattern IS a fixed geometry, and a trick that
# reshapes every throw conflicts with it directly. The combination audit measured exactly
# that: Behind the Back under Columns clears -13.6px, because a column comes down where it
# left and a behind-the-back throw releases from across the body.
